    "evidence_text": "Reverse engineering of TLX oncogenic transcriptional networks identifies RUNX1 as tumor suppressor in T-ALL\n\nConsistent with these results, we identified recurrent somatic loss-of-function mutations in RUNX1 in human T-ALL.\n\nMoreover, LIN28B accelerated tumor initiation and enhanced proliferation which led to a shortened overall survival.\n\nCancer stem cell eradication is thought to be crucial for successful anticancer therapy.\n\nWe generated Tspan3 knockout mice that were born without overt defects.\n\nEarly chromatin unfolding by RUNX1: a molecular explanation for differential requirements during specification versus maintenance of the hematopoietic gene expression program.\n\nAt the cellular level, development progresses through successive regulatory states, each characterized by their specific gene expression profile.\n\nBy contrast, chromatin reorganization of Csf1r requires prior expression of PU.1 together with RUNX1 binding.\n\nThus, Notch1 and loss of Tcf1 functionally act as collaborating oncogenic events.\n\nModulation of cellular and viral promoters by mutant human p53 proteins found in tumor cells.\n\nAs expected, expression of the wild-type p53 inhibited promoter function.\n\nThe viral promoters were also activated, although to a somewhat lesser extent.",
